# Disable two crash-looping stock-vendor HALs by stopping them the instant init
# brings them up (and again if init schedules a restart), breaking the respawn loop.
# Same approach as disable-ssdaemon.rc — their .rc files live on the stock /vendor
# partition we ride and can't edit, but /product/etc/init is parsed AFTER /vendor so
# these stop triggers win. Both stops are belt-and-suspenders because these abort
# during init (faster than the modem ssdaemons), so catch them in running OR restarting.
#
# 1. gto_secure_element_aidl_service (Thales/Gemalto eSE HAL): aborts every ~5s with
#    "Check failed: status == STATUS_OK (status=-3)" — the SE stack can't initialize on
#    this port. Each abort flips sys.init.updatable_crashing=1, which runs
#    flags_health_check (RescueParty) on a loop, continuously resetting feature flags
#    and flooding the log with ~3500 AVC denials. Disabling stops that storm and the
#    battery drain. eSE is already non-functional (it's broken); basic NFC + host card
#    emulation (HCE) are unaffected. It IS VINTF-declared
#    (/vendor/etc/vintf/manifest/android.hardware.secure_element.xml), but a
#    declared-not-served HAL is not a runtime boot gate — clients fail gracefully.
#    (Build/RE: confirm NFC still works and there's no boot regression.)
#
# 2. ifaaaidl (vendor.zte.fingerprint.ifaaaidl-service): China-market IFAA
#    biometric-pay attestation HAL, restarting hundreds of times per boot. Not in any
#    VINTF manifest, unused on LineageOS. The working Goodix UDFPS (ztecmdaidl) is a
#    separate service and is unaffected.

on property:init.svc.gto_secure_element_aidl_service=running
    stop gto_secure_element_aidl_service

on property:init.svc.gto_secure_element_aidl_service=restarting
    stop gto_secure_element_aidl_service

on property:init.svc.ifaaaidl=running
    stop ifaaaidl

on property:init.svc.ifaaaidl=restarting
    stop ifaaaidl
